    {
      "number": 4,
      "id": "I_kwDOSm5Pfs8AAAABHZCNgA",
      "title": "To link ICON to AINETOPS, what is specific here?",
      "url": "https://github.com/billwuqin/ICON-problem-statement/issues/4",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "QiufangMa",
      "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
      "assignees": [
        "billwuqin"
      ],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "- This ICON work is linked/specific to AI4NET? The AI guardrail is for any AI agent, right? What is specific to networking here?\n782\t   This section outlines key areas where standardization is required to\n783\t   support the design, implementation, and operation of Agent\n784\t   Observability,Intervention and Control in autonomous networks.  The\n785\t   intent is to identify foundational areas that require align with\n786\t   network management technologies developed in IETF OPS Area and drive\n787\t   network automation moving toward AI Driven Network Operation.\n",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-02T03:26:18Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-03T08:39:29Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-07-03T08:39:29Z",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
      "number": 5,
      "id": "I_kwDOSm5Pfs8AAAABHZDz9Q",
      "title": "Define the notions",
      "url": "https://github.com/billwuqin/ICON-problem-statement/issues/5",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "QiufangMa",
      "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "input guardrail\noutput guardrail\nsub-agent\nmalicious abuse?",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-02T03:32:12Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-03T08:39:45Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-07-03T08:39:44Z",
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "billwuqin",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "Benoit Said:\n\" \n- If I had to implement this, I would put the guardrail directly in the AI agent itself, no? Something like claude.md file\nOk, the question would then be: can we trust the AI agent (and sometime the answer if no)\n- if I look at AI guardrail\nGuardrails are checks that run alongside your agents to catch bad input or bad output \u2014 without necessarily involving your main (expensive/slow) model. The canonical example: you have a smart, costly model answering customer support questions. You don't want people abusing it to do their math homework. So you attach a cheap/fast guardrail check that flags that behavior and stops the expensive agent from wasting tokens on it.\nI don't see this notion of input/output in the draft; it's important because the checks are different\n\"",
          "createdAt": "2026-07-03T02:51:03Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-07-03T03:12:03Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "billwuqin",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "Yes, AI guardrail can be deployed directly in the AI agent itself, but it doesn't scale. You can not guarantee each agent will be a good cityzen.\nAlternatively, we can deploy AI guardrail at the proxy or Agent gateway to support guardrail for multiple agents.",
          "createdAt": "2026-07-03T03:15:12Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-07-03T03:17:53Z"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
